//cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/30f03274/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz00MzI7aD0yNDMpOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1qcGVnKQ/up/dt/2026/11/Hantavirus-infections_Review-examines-oral-signs-and-risks-for-dental-practice.jpg 432w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/47a6b85c/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz04NjM7aD00ODUpOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1qcGVnKQ/up/dt/2026/11/Hantavirus-infections_Review-examines-oral-signs-and-risks-for-dental-practice.jpg 863w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/23e80461/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz0xMTUwO2g9NjQ2KTpzaGFycGVuKGxldmVsPTApOm91dHB1dChmb3JtYXQ9anBlZyk/up/dt/2026/11/Hantavirus-infections_Review-examines-oral-signs-and-risks-for-dental-practice.jpg 1150w\" type=\"image/jpeg\"><img src=\"https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/23e80461/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz0xMTUwO2g9NjQ2KTpzaGFycGVuKGxldmVsPTApOm91dHB1dChmb3JtYXQ9anBlZyk/up/dt/2026/11/Hantavirus-infections_Review-examines-oral-signs-and-risks-for-dental-practice.jpg\" alt=\"Hantavirus infections: Review examines oral signs and risks for dental practice\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\t</picture> The Minister for Science and Culture of Lower Saxony, Falko Mohrs (middle), tries out a dental simulation unit at Hannover Medical School under the guidance of Prof. Felix Kunz (left), director of the department of orthodontics, and Prof. Michael Eisenburger (right) of the department of prosthodontics and biomedical materials science. (Image: Karin Kaiser/Hannover Medical School) <a href=\"https://www.dental-tribune.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"> <picture><source srcset=\"https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/795224c2/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz00MDA7aD00MDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD13ZWJwKQ/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif 400w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/ce1d6420/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz0yMDA7aD0yMDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD13ZWJwKQ/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif 200w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/7a11fe25/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz0zMDA7aD0zMDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD13ZWJwKQ/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif 300w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/9e3b5491/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz02MDA7aD02MDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD13ZWJwKQ/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif 600w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/0ffdc2e5/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz04MDA7aD04MDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD13ZWJwKQ/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif 800w\" type=\"image/webp\"> <source srcset=\"https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/7313e142/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz00MDA7aD00MDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1naWYp/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif 400w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/9790625d/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz0yMDA7aD0yMDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1naWYp/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif 200w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/db4e45be/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz0zMDA7aD0zMDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1naWYp/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif 300w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/efe38b3b/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz02MDA7aD02MDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1naWYp/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif 600w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/ae55a8d2/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz04MDA7aD04MDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1naWYp/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif 800w\" type=\"image/gif\"><img src=\"https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/ae55a8d2/cmVzaXplLWNyb3Aodz04MDA7aD04MDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1naWYp/wp-content/themes/dt/images/dt-user.gif\" alt=\"Dental Tribune International\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\t</picture> <p>Dental Tribune International</p> </a> <p>Mon. 16. November 2026</p> share save <h2><p>HANOVER, Germany: Hannover Medical School (MHH) has completed the modernisation and expansion of its dental simulation facilities, providing students with 92 digital training units across two simulation rooms. Supported by an investment of more than €13 million from the state of Lower Saxony, the facilities at the university’s centre for dentistry, oral medicine and maxillofacial medicine enable students to practise clinical procedures and digital workflows in a simulated setting.</p>\n</h2> <p>The second simulation room was officially opened on 3 November, following the completion of the first in August last year. According to MHH, the investment has created one of Germany’s most advanced simulation facilities for undergraduate dental education.</p>\n<p>The facilities are primarily used by the department of conservative dentistry, periodontics and preventive dentistry and the department of prosthodontics and biomedical materials science. They combine conventional practical training on dental phantom heads with digital technologies designed to reproduce different stages of clinical treatment.</p>\n<h2>Integrating digital workflows into dental education</h2>\n<p>A central feature of the facilities is a teaching network connecting all the simulation units. Instructors can transmit demonstrations from microscopes, cameras and computer workstations directly to individual student monitors, allowing students to follow procedures simultaneously throughout the training rooms.</p>\n<picture><source srcset=\"https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/e1da6c85/cmVzaXplKHc9MTYwMDtoPTkwMCk6c2hhcnBlbihsZXZlbD0wKTpvdXRwdXQoZm9ybWF0PXdlYnAp/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg 1600w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/3b3c4f0e/cmVzaXplKHc9ODAwO2g9NDUwKTpzaGFycGVuKGxldmVsPTApOm91dHB1dChmb3JtYXQ9d2VicCk/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg 800w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/58b6c8ef/cmVzaXplKHc9MTIwMDtoPTY3NSk6c2hhcnBlbihsZXZlbD0wKTpvdXRwdXQoZm9ybWF0PXdlYnAp/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg 1200w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/a38bb86b/cmVzaXplKHc9MjQwMDtoPTEzNTApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD13ZWJwKQ/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg 2400w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/da9db8d6/cmVzaXplKHc9MzIwMDtoPTE4MDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD13ZWJwKQ/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg 3200w\" type=\"image/webp\"><source srcset=\"https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/83ffe97d/cmVzaXplKHc9MTYwMDtoPTkwMCk6c2hhcnBlbihsZXZlbD0wKTpvdXRwdXQoZm9ybWF0PWpwZWcp/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg 1600w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/9c90be74/cmVzaXplKHc9ODAwO2g9NDUwKTpzaGFycGVuKGxldmVsPTApOm91dHB1dChmb3JtYXQ9anBlZyk/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg 800w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/f9f254ae/cmVzaXplKHc9MTIwMDtoPTY3NSk6c2hhcnBlbihsZXZlbD0wKTpvdXRwdXQoZm9ybWF0PWpwZWcp/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg 1200w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/0f0a9ffe/cmVzaXplKHc9MjQwMDtoPTEzNTApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1qcGVnKQ/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg 2400w, https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/bf14131b/cmVzaXplKHc9MzIwMDtoPTE4MDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1qcGVnKQ/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg 3200w\" type=\"image/jpeg\"><img src=\"https://cdn.dental-tribune.com/dti/0001/bf14131b/cmVzaXplKHc9MzIwMDtoPTE4MDApOnNoYXJwZW4obGV2ZWw9MCk6b3V0cHV0KGZvcm1hdD1qcGVnKQ/up/dt/2026/11/2025-01-03_Hilfiker-Kleiner_MHH-Praesidentin-800px.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\">\n\t</picture>\n<p>Hannover Medical School President Prof. Denise Hilfiker-Kleiner. (Image: Hannover Medical School)</p>\n\n<p>In specialised simulation sessions, students can use intra-oral scans of individual patient cases to practise treatment virtually. According to MHH, this form of preclinical training enables students to learn a range of procedures, including complex treatments such as root canal therapy, in a resource-efficient manner.</p>\n<p>The facilities also enable students to work through the successive stages of digitally supported prosthodontic treatment within a single training environment. Students first prepare teeth on phantom heads for crowns or bridges and then scan the prepared teeth using intra-oral scanners. They subsequently design and manufacture the restorations using CAD/CAM processes and can then fit the completed restorations to the prepared teeth on the phantom models.</p>\n<h2>Responding to changing educational requirements</h2>\n<p>MHH’s previous phantom training facilities had been in use since 1993. Their replacement and expansion were intended to accommodate both technological developments and changes introduced under Germany’s dental licensing regulations, which have restructured dental education and examinations and increased requirements for small-group teaching and student supervision.</p>\n<p>In <a href=\"https://www.mhh.de/en/presse/mhh-insight/homepage-news-detailed-view/new-simulation-rooms-for-dentistry-education\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">a university press release</a>, MHH President Prof. Denise Hilfiker-Kleiner highlighted the role of simulation-based education in preparing students for clinical responsibilities. She stated that the new facilities support training in complex procedures under realistic conditions and added that they will enhance the quality of dental education and contribute to patient safety.</p>\n<p>More broadly, the modernised facilities are a response to the growing need to better align undergraduate dental education with the ongoing digital advancements in contemporary clinical practice. A similar development was announced in October at The University of Texas at San Antonio School of Dentistry in the US, which has introduced <a href=\"https://www.dental-tribune.com/news/us-dental-school-introduces-complete-digital-workflow-from-first-year/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">a complete digital workflow</a> for restorative dentistry from the first year of study.</p>","kind":"full"}